# The studio shot of car_studio.scene with gloss metallic paint instead of
# the model's satin. Everything else is the same (keep the two in step).
#
# The file's paint (Material.043) is a metal of roughness 0.51 under a 21%
# clear coat: the softboxes spread into broad soft gradients. Real metallic
# paint is two layers: a base of metal flakes in color, fairly rough (they
# point every which way, so the color glows over a wide angle), and on top a
# thick clear varnish, smooth as glass. The coat gives the sharp reflections
# of the softboxes, crisp lines that follow the body; the base gives the
# colored sheen under them. That is the "wet" look of a new car.
#
# Studio car shot: a cyclorama and four softboxes, the classic car-photography
# setup, around the Porsche 992 GT3 RS ("porsche-992-gt3-rs" on Sketchfab,
# saved as models/external/porsche_992_gt3_rs.glb; not in git).
#
# The car is the mesh line at the bottom (its replace options only fit this model): any car whose wheels sit on y = 0,
# centered on the origin with its length along z and its nose towards +z,
# drops in (turn or move it with rotate_y / translate otherwise). The camera
# is at the front right three-quarter view, and the cyclorama is turned
# (rotate_y 40) to face it.
#
# The lights, and why:
#  - Paint is a mirror under a clear coat: on a car you mostly see what the
#    body reflects, not the light falling on it. Lighting a car is choosing
#    what it reflects, so the lights are big shapes placed where the camera
#    sees them in the body.
#  - Overhead "flying flat" (3 x 7 m): bigger than the car and straight above
#    it, so the bonnet, roof and the top of the wings reflect one long, even
#    highlight that follows their curvature. It also gives the soft contact
#    shadow that sets the car on the floor.
#  - Side strip (8 x 1 m, lying along the car) low on the right, behind it:
#    the right flank mirrors it as a line running along the doors (a mirror
#    shows what lies opposite the camera across the normal, so for a flank
#    seen from the front, that is behind and to the side); how the line bends
#    shows the shoulder and the wheel arches.
#  - Front-left strip (0.7 x 3 m, vertical): lights the nose and puts a
#    vertical highlight on the front bumper and wing corner, so the front
#    doesn't merge with the dark studio.
#  - Wash: a low strip hidden behind the car, aimed at the wall, puts a glow
#    on the backdrop just behind the car so its dark top and rear wing stand
#    out against it (separation).
#  - Everything else is black (background 0): the dark between the
#    highlights is what draws the body's shape. Grids (spread) keep each box
#    on the car instead of the backdrop, which falls off into a gradient.
#  - The cyclorama curves into the floor, so there is no horizon line and the
#    car stands in a seamless space; its semi-gloss floor shows a soft
#    reflection of the car.

image_width  1920
aspect_ratio 16/9
samples_per_pixel 256
max_depth    16

# Low (1 m, below the roofline) and on a longer lens: the car fills the
# frame and looks more imposing than seen from eye height
camera lookfrom 5.2 1.0 6.1  lookat 0 0.6 0.1  vfov 25

background 0 0 0
tonemap    aces
exposure   0

# Backdrop: mid-grey, slightly glossy
material cyc principled color 0.35 0.35 0.35 roughness 0.2
cyclorama width 26 front 14 back 4.5 height 9 radius 3.5 cyc  rotate_y 40

# Softboxes: emissive behind a honeycomb grid (spread = full cone angle)
material overhead emissive 10 10 10 spread 100
material strip    emissive 10 10 10 spread 80
softbox center 0 4.2 0      aim 0 0 0        size 3 7    overhead
softbox center 4.2 0.7 -5.5 aim 0 0.7 -4     size 8 1.0  strip
softbox center -3.2 1.5 4.5 aim 0 0.5 1.5    size 0.7 3  strip
# Behind the car, on the floor and facing the wall: a glow on the backdrop
material wash     emissive 30 30 30 spread 120
softbox center -3.2 0.2 -3.9 aim -5.1 2 -6.1  size 4 0.3  wash
# Wheel light: a metal rim shows only what it reflects, and a spoke face
# (normal pointing out of the car's side) seen from the camera mirrors the
# floor beside and behind the car, which is dark: the spokes went brown
# with a bright rim. A low strip there, along the car and facing it, is what
# the spoke faces see. Car photographers do the same with a low softbox.
material wheel    emissive 10 10 10 spread 90
softbox center 2.4 0.35 -1.9 aim 0.9 0.35 -1.5  size 3.2 0.5  wheel

# The model's windows are see-through shells for a real-time viewer: dark
# purple, hit by 41% of the rays, so they looked pink from the overhead
# light's reflection. Real glass instead: a thin sheet that reflects by
# Fresnel (about 4% head-on, a mirror at grazing angles) and lets the rest
# straight through. Each window is two shells (outside and inside), so the
# tint is per shell. The headlight covers get clear glass too.
material window    thin_glass 1.5 color 0.92 0.95 0.93
material headlight thin_glass 1.5

# Same olive as the file, as a flake base under a full, mirror-smooth coat
material paint principled color 0.254 0.238 0.162 metallic 1 roughness 0.35 clearcoat 1 clearcoat_roughness 0.02

# Rims (Material.058 in the file: gold, metallic 0.89, roughness 0.23, coat).
# Copper instead. A metal's color is its reflectance seen head-on (F0); real
# copper's is about 0.95 0.64 0.54 (linear): light and salmon-pink. Bare
# copper tarnishes, so copper parts get a clear lacquer: the coat.
material rim principled color 0.95 0.64 0.54 metallic 1 roughness 0.25 clearcoat 1 clearcoat_roughness 0.03
# Or copper paint ("burnished copper"): darker, browner, satin under a coat
#material rim principled color 0.55 0.27 0.17 metallic 1 roughness 0.35 clearcoat 1 clearcoat_roughness 0.03

mesh ../models/external/porsche_992_gt3_rs.glb  replace Material.038_64 window  replace Material.038_63 window  replace Material.038_49 headlight  replace Material.058 rim  replace Material.043 paint  rotate_y 180   # this model faces -z
